From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 06:16:42 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] script: Cancel a suspended media fetch that nothing might
 resume

A media element resumes a suspended fetch only once its player needs
data again, and nothing cancels a fetch when its fetch context is
dropped. A suspended fetch that nothing resumes holds its connection,
and up to an HTTP/2 stream window in net, until net gives up on it. The
fetch listener keeps the element alive while its fetch runs, so it is
not collected meanwhile either.

The fetch context goes away, or its fetch is left alone, on these
paths:
- a fetch that replaces it, after a seek or a restart, cancels it;
- the load algorithm, run by load() and a new src or srcObject, cancels
  it, and so do the failure and fatal steps;
- the element cannot be collected while its fetch runs;
- an element that is paused outside a document, after it was removed
  from its document or because it was never inserted, as with
  new Audio(), might never need data again;
- a document that is no longer fully active, such as one navigated away
  from and kept in the session history, has its timers and media
  suspended;
- aborting a document, on a navigation, window.stop(), document.open()
  or when the document is destroyed, terminates its fetch group, which
  cancels the fetch but leaves the element waiting for it to resume;
- when the pipeline exits, nothing cancels the fetch.

Do not suspend the fetch of an element that is paused outside a
document, or whose document is not fully active; back off as before.
Cancel a suspended fetch, to restart it once the player needs data
again, when its element is paused outside a document, which the steps
for a removed element do, and when its document is aborted, stops
being fully active or exits; for these, the document keeps weak
references to the media elements that suspended their fetch. Also
cancel a fetch that is still running when its fetch context is
dropped. A fetch whose response ended is marked as such when the
element handles the end, so that none of these cancellations send
anything for it; if the end was not handled, because the element had
moved on to another load or lost its player, dropping the context sends
one cancellation, which net ignores.

Fork note: meant for upstream. Drop once upstream has it.

Testing: no test; a fetch is only suspended once the 50 MiB queue of the
GStreamer player is full. See Validation.

Validation: the setup of the previous patch. A suspended element removed from its document 6 s into the load: the server sees the connection closed at 6.0 s, with or without range support; without this patch, on an earlier revision of the previous patch, at 33.5 s through the timeout with range support, and only when servoshell exited without. A suspended element whose load() runs with another src, and a document that navigates away, close it at 6.0 s. `./mach test-wpt --release --processes 6 --timeout-multiplier 2` html/semantics/embedded-content/media-elements, the-video-element and html/canvas/element/manual/drawing-images-to-the-canvas with the series: 451 tests, 446 as expected; the other five behave the same on the parent commit of the series when run alone: autoplay-default-permissions-policy.https.sub.html passes where a timeout is expected (3/3 on both), media_fragment_seek.html fails a subtest (3/3 on both), src_object_blob.html times out 1 of 3 runs on both, video_timeupdate_on_seek.html passes a subtest that is expected to time out, and load-removes-queued-error-event.html hangs intermittently because GStreamer never reports the metadata of the 539-byte sound_0.mp3 (3 of 8 runs with the series, 4 of 8 without, alternating). `./mach check --ohos` passes at each commit; `./mach test-tidy` reports only the known rustls advisories.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; implemented, tested and measured by the agent)
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; 2026-10-22 sync -- mechanical conflict resolution, no semantic change: the `MediaElementWeakRef` import next to upstream's `TracedScriptResult` (#48855))
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; 2026-10-29 sync -- mechanical conflict resolution, no semantic change: the document's list of media elements with a suspended fetch no longer sits next to patch 65's keep-alive list, which upstream's (#48979) replaced)
